  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 251 AM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nPast Monday, a pattern change is in store as a trough advances \nacross the northern Plains into Canada. This will bring us multiple \nweather concerns of heat, thunderstorm chances, and heavy downpours \nTuesday and Wednesday.\n\nAs the midlevel ridge collapses eastward, low level winds will turn \nsoutherly to southwesterly, advecting in warm and moist air late \nMonday night through Tuesday. While temperatures will be hot, with \nTuesday's highs in the mid 80s to mid 90s, heat indices will not be \nas oppressive as last week's heat due to the potential for showers \nand storms throughout the day. At this time, heat indices are \nexpected to remain below headline criteria over much of the area, \nwith only parts of northeast Missouri and far southeast Iowa having \nmoderate chances of exceeding heat indices of 100 degrees (20-60%).\n\nAcross the northern half of the area, rainfall and thunderstorm \npotential will be a greater concern than heat. Multiple waves \nassociated with the northern Plains/southern Canada trough will pass \nacross the area Tuesday and Wednesday, leading to multiple rounds of \nrain. While some showers will be possible (15-30%) Tuesday morning, \nthe bulk of the rainfall will occur Tuesday afternoon through \nWednesday as a cold front slowly sags through the region. While the \ntiming remains consistent across much guidance, rainfall totals do \nnot. NBM 1-inch exceedance probabilities over 48 hours are low at 20-\n50%. However, shear parallel to the front suggests that training \nthunderstorms will be possible, leading to the potential for more \nlocalized heavy rain. CAM guidance has caught onto this potential, \nas can be seen in the REFS's 1-4 inch 24-hour rainfall north of \nInterstate 80 in Iowa. While recent dryness may help limit \nwidespread flooding concerns, heavy rainfall could lead to a \nlocalized flooding threat. As a result, WPC has issued a Slight, \nlevel 2 of 4, risk for flash flooding for Tuesday.\n\nIn addition to the potential for training, convective parameters \nsuggest that there is some potential for isolated severe storms \novernight Tuesday through Wednesday morning. Due to a more \nmulticellular storm mode, damaging winds and large hail will be the \nprimary threats. SPC has issued a Marginal, level 1 of 5, risk for \nsevere thunderstorms.\n\nOn Wednesday, showers and storms will slowly exit the area as the \ncold front pushes through. Model trends have shown this slowing \ndown, with some not having the front exit until late Wednesday \nevening or even Thursday morning. As a result, Wednesday, while \nstill hot, will begin the transition to another period of cooler but \nabove normal temperatures. Flow aloft will also become more zonal \nonce the front moves out, though multiple impulses will lead to \nadditional chances for showers, storms, and some heat towards the \nweekend.",
